    ]
  },
  "expected_benefit": "Search will favor designs with higher expected (robust) performance and larger safety margin from the failure hyperplane, reducing the frequency of zero-score failures and shrinking the nominal–robust gap. The stronger cliff_margin weight will act as a stable surrogate to counteract MC noise in early iterations.",
  "validation_plan": "On the next iteration, expect to see increased population mean and/or best robust_performance_mc (currently mean 0.3900, best 0.4724), a larger average cliff_margin (currently 0.0608) and fewer candidates with negative cliff_margin or zero robust/nominal scores.",
  "confidence": 0.72
}
</decision>

Rationale (brief, evidence-based):
- The Analyzer flags limited objective signal as a key issue: robust_performance_mc mean = 0.4536 (std 0.0418; improved from mean 0.39,std 0.0985) and best ≈ 0.4995 — far from ideal 1.0. The population is narrowing quickly (reduced std) which suggests premature convergence.
- Safety still violated for a few candidates: cliff_margin mean = 0.1021 (std 0.0476) with worst = -0.0230 (some negative margins).
- The current objective set lacks a noise‑free nominal signal and an explicit center-proximity proxy for robustness. Adding nominal_performance (exact, 0 beyond failure) and center_distance (minimize) should provide complementary, lower-noise guidance, while increasing cliff_margin weight will discourage any remaining failures.

